[38;5;231m⏺[39m This looks [1mbounded[0m — one file, one existing flow ([38;5;153mapp.js:4[39m), one caller — so I'll present a short design in chat
  rather than write a spec. But there's a question I can't answer from the code.

  [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m is called at [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m from the form submit handler, which only has the username and
  password the user typed. There is no [38;5;153muserId[39m available at that call site — nothing in this repo produces one. So "add a
  userId parameter" has a fork in it:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153muserId[39m is something the [3mcaller[0m supplies, or something [3mlogin itself[0m produces.

  - [1mIt's the identity the server returns.[0m Then it isn't a parameter at all — the stub already returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, [39m
    [38;5;153muser: username }[39m, and "track who logged in" means logging the identity out of the result. Cheapest, no signature
    change, nothing else to build. But it only tells you who logged in [3msuccessfully[0m — a failed attempt has no userId.
  - [1mIt's a client-side correlation ID[0m generated per attempt and passed in, so a login attempt can be traced whether or
    not it succeeds. This does change the signature and adds an ID-generation step; the caller becomes responsible for
    creating it. Changing the signature is cheap now (one caller, same file) and gets expensive once anything else calls
    [38;5;153mlogin[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m just means the username.[0m Then the parameter is redundant — [38;5;153musername[39m is already the parameter — and the real
    change is what gets logged, not the signature.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the third reading is almost certainly what "track who logged in" means, and it needs no new
  parameter. But if you want failed attempts traceable too, the correlation ID is the only option that covers them.
